    "131": {
      "def_id": 131,
      "item_meta": {
        "name": [
          {
            "Ident": [
              "core",
              0
            ]
          },
          {
            "Ident": [
              "mem",
              0
            ]
          },
          {
            "Ident": [
              "maybe_dangling",
              0
            ]
          },
          {
            "Ident": [
              "MaybeDangling",
              0
            ]
          }
        ],
        "span": {
          "data": {
            "file_id": 129,
            "beg": {
              "line": 74,
              "col": 0
            },
            "end": {
              "line": 74,
              "col": 35
            }
          },
          "generated_from_span": null
        },
        "source_text": null,
        "attr_info": {
          "attributes": [
            {
              "DocComment": " Allows wrapped [references] and [boxes] to dangle."
            },
            {
              "DocComment": ""
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " That is, if a reference (or a `Box`) is wrapped in `MaybeDangling` (including when in a"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " (nested) field of a compound type wrapped in `MaybeDangling`), it does not have to follow"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " pointer aliasing rules or be dereferenceable."
            },
            {
              "DocComment": ""
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " This can be useful when the value can become dangling while the function holding it is still"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " executing (particularly in concurrent code). As a somewhat absurd example, consider this code:"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": ""
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " ```rust,no_run"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " #![feature(box_as_ptr)]"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " # use std::alloc::{dealloc, Layout};"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " # use std::mem;"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": ""
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " let mut boxed = Box::new(0_u32);"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " let ptr = Box::as_mut_ptr(&mut boxed);"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": ""
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " // Safety: the pointer comes from a box and thus was allocated before; `box` is not used afterwards"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " unsafe { dealloc(ptr.cast(), Layout::new::<u32>()) };"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": ""
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " mem::forget(boxed); // <-- this is UB!"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " ```"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": ""
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " Even though the `Box`'s destructor is not run (and thus we don't have a double free bug), this"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " code is still UB. This is because when moving `boxed` into `forget`, its validity invariants"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " are asserted, causing UB since the `Box` is dangling. The safety comment is as such wrong, as"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " moving the `boxed` variable as part of the `forget` call *is* a use."
            },
            {
              "DocComment": ""
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " To fix this we could use `MaybeDangling`:"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": ""
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " ```rust,no_run"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " #![feature(maybe_dangling, box_as_ptr)]"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " # use std::alloc::{dealloc, Layout};"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " # use std::mem::{self, MaybeDangling};"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": ""
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " let mut boxed = MaybeDangling::new(Box::new(0_u32));"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " let ptr = Box::as_mut_ptr(boxed.as_mut());"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": ""
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " // Safety: the pointer comes from a box and thus was allocated before; `box` is not used afterwards"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " unsafe { dealloc(ptr.cast(), Layout::new::<u32>()) };"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": ""
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " mem::forget(boxed); // <-- this is OK!"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " ```"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": ""
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " Note that the bit pattern must still be valid for the wrapped type. That is, [references]"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " (and [boxes]) still must be aligned and non-null."
            },
            {
              "DocComment": ""
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " Additionally note that safe code can still assume that the inner value in a `MaybeDangling` is"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " **not** dangling -- functions like [`as_ref`] and [`into_inner`] are safe. It is not sound to"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " return a dangling reference in a `MaybeDangling` to safe code. However, it *is* sound"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " to hold such values internally inside your code -- and there's no way to do that without"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " this type. Note that other types can use this type and thus get the same effect; in particular,"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " [`ManuallyDrop`] will use `MaybeDangling`."
            },
            {
              "DocComment": ""
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " Note that `MaybeDangling` doesn't prevent drops from being run, which can lead to UB if the"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " drop observes a dangling value. If you need to prevent drops from being run use [`ManuallyDrop`]"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " instead."
            },
            {
              "DocComment": ""
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " [references]: prim@reference"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " [boxes]: ../../std/boxed/struct.Box.html"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " [`into_inner`]: MaybeDangling::into_inner"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " [`as_ref`]: MaybeDangling::as_ref"
            },
            {
              "DocComment": " [`ManuallyDrop`]: crate::mem::ManuallyDrop"
            }
          ],
          "inline": null,
          "rename": null,
          "public": true
        },
        "is_local": false,
        "opacity": "Foreign",
        "lang_item": "maybe_dangling"
